        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 25,
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.778,
            0.648,
            0.904
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "There are situations in which forces do not act parallel or opposite to each other but act at an angle to each other. You will learn in higher grades how to calculate the net force in such cases. Also, there are situations where equal and opposite forces are applied to the two ends of an extended object which make the object rotate (Fig. 6.7). For example, applying equal and opposite forces to a handlebar or a tap makes it turn. You will also learn about this in higher grades."
            }
          ]
        },
